PROPOSAL — not shipped, not applied by `weld add cron`.

Target: capabilities/http/files/serve.go.tmpl (generated internal/app/serve.go)
Owner: the http capability.

Why it is not applied here: the plain serve command owns its own file, and cron
must not edit it. The scheduler only starts inside the long-running serve
command; help, version and every short command never reach this code.

Prerequisite: runServe today calls httpserver.Serve, which blocks until the
server fails and installs no signal handling, so there is no graceful-shutdown
point to hang the scheduler's Stop on. Wiring cron into the plain serve command
therefore also needs the http capability to serve under a context that a signal
cancels. The shape below assumes that change; without it, only Register/Start can
be added and Stop would never run on Ctrl-C.

Proposed edit (inside runServe, after the logger is built and before serving):

    ctx, stop := signal.NotifyContext(context.Background(), os.Interrupt, syscall.SIGTERM)
    defer stop()

    location, err := cfg.Cron.Location()
    if err != nil {
        return err
    }
    scheduler := cron.New(logger, cron.WithLocation(location))
    // Register the application's jobs here, on the scheduler, before Start.
    scheduler.Start(ctx)
    defer func() {
        shutdownCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second)
        defer cancel()
        if err := scheduler.Stop(shutdownCtx); err != nil && !errors.Is(err, context.Canceled) {
            logger.Error("__name__ cron shutdown failed", "error", err)
        }
    }()

    return httpserver.Serve(ctx, logger, listenAddr, httpserver.Handler())

Imports to add: "context", "os/signal", "syscall", and
"__module__/internal/cron" (aliased cron).

The schedule itself comes from config.Cron.Timezone, so the http capability does
not need a new setting: cron.timezone defaults to UTC (see internal/config/cron.go).
